{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PtsYdJ2LjZp3Z7dAkpPzFy","type":"tool_result","content":"29:**Also flagged:** B3 and B4 disagree on the early spacing ramp. B3 says Ch 3 = 10%, Ch 4 = 15%, then 20–25%. B4's restatement says Ch 4–5 ≈ 10%, Ch 6–8 ≈ 15%, Ch 9–13 ≈ 20%. B3 is the dedicated stage and its schedule is adopted here; B4's table is a generic restatement of the skill's Part 10 example and is superseded.\n50:**The spine.** The book has one structural argument, stated three times at increasing altitude: *the control loop*. Chapter 3 introduces it as the animating idea of the cluster. Chapter 6 instantiates it in the workload controllers. Chapter 15 generalizes it to a Git repository — and that recognition, not a fifth list to memorize, is the book's **primary Zenith**. The whole of Part IV's ordering (packaging → delivery → debugging) exists to set that beat up: Argo CD consumes Helm charts, so Helm must land first.\n52:The **secondary Zenith** is Chapter 17's extension-points synthesis, where CRI (Ch 2), CNI (Ch 9), CSI (Ch 11), and CRDs (Ch 6) resolve into a single pluggability story with back-bearings to all four.\n62:- **One deliberate forward reference: StatefulSet.** It appears in Ch 6 with its sibling workload resources, five chapters before PersistentVolumes are defined, because it belongs taxonomically with Deployment and DaemonSet. Ch 6 teaches *why it is different*; Ch 11 closes the loop with the PV pairing.\n113:- **Notes**: `ch03-fig02` is the book's most reused figure — Ch 6, Ch 15, and Ch 17 all call back to its shape. Design it once, deliberately, for re-presentation at three later altitudes.\n147:### Chapter 6 — Fleets, Not Vessels\n156:- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch06-fig01-deployment-replicaset-pod-ownership`, `ch06-fig02-rolling-update-maxsurge-maxunavailable`, `ch06-fig03-recreate-vs-rolling`, `ch06-fig04-workload-resource-decision-tree`, `ch06-fig05-statefulset-vs-deployment-identity`, `ch06-zenith-control-loop-instantiated`\n167:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 5 (Pod, requests/limits), Ch 6 (workload resources creating Pods)\n169:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]** — from Ch 4–6. Anchors: labels (Ch 4) now doing node selection rather than Pod selection; requests (Ch 5) as the filter input; the controller (Ch 6) that produced the unscheduled Pod\n197:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 4 (labels, selectors), Ch 5 (Pod network namespace), Ch 6 (Pod churn under controllers)\n199:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]**, from Ch 5–8, ≥4-back floor satisfied by Ch 5 (Pod shared network namespace — exactly four back). Additional anchors: selectors (Ch 4) now joining Service→Pod; ReplicaSet churn (Ch 6) as the reason a stable name is needed\n214:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]**, from Ch 6–9, ≥4-back floor satisfied by Ch 4 (labels/selectors, now selecting NetworkPolicy subjects) or Ch 5 (Pod IP). Additional anchor: the Service-type ladder (Ch 9) against what Ingress actually replaces\n226:- **Covers**: **D2.4** — volume types (emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret, projected, ephemeral); PersistentVolume; PersistentVolumeClaim; StorageClass; static vs dynamic provisioning; binding; reclaim policies (Retain/Delete/Recycle); access modes (RWO/ROX/RWX/RWOP); CSI; StatefulSet + PV pairing (loop closed from Ch 6)\n227:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 4 (objects, ConfigMap/Secret as volume sources), Ch 5 (Pod volumes), Ch 6 (StatefulSet, introduced)\n229:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]**, from Ch 6–10, ≥4-back floor satisfied by Ch 6 (StatefulSet — five back) and Ch 5 (Pod volume mounts). Additional anchor: ConfigMap/Secret (Ch 4) as volume types the reader already met as objects\n233:- **Notes**: Blocking gaps G11 (CSI), G12 (volume types other than PV/PVC). **Closes the book's one deliberate forward reference** — `ch11-fig05` is the back half of the Ch 6 StatefulSet cross-bearing and must be built as a reciprocal pair with `ch06-fig05`. CSI planted here is the third pluggable interface resolving in Ch 17.\n272:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 4 (manifests, `kubectl apply`), Ch 6 (workload resources, rollback)\n274:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]**, from Ch 9–13, ≥4-back floor satisfied by Ch 4 (object anatomy, `apply`) and Ch 6 (workload resources, revision/rollback). Additional anchor: Ch 6 rollback vs Helm rollback — different mechanisms, same word\n287:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 (the control loop), Ch 6 (controllers, rolling update mechanics), Ch 14 (Helm charts as a manifest source)\n289:- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. ≥4-back floor satisfied several times over. Mandatory anchors: **the control loop (Ch 3)** — the retrieval that *is* the Zenith; rolling-update mechanics (Ch 6) now given strategy vocabulary; Helm charts (Ch 14) as Argo's input; ConfigMap/Secret (Ch 4) as twelve-factor factor III; drift detection against `spec`/`status` (Ch 4)\n304:- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. Mandatory anchors: the Ch 13 handoff, retrieved as the chapter's opening move; Pod phases and probes (Ch 5); Service→EndpointSlice→Pod path (Ch 9) as the \"is my Service even selecting anything\" check; StatefulSet identity (Ch 6) and its PVC pairing (Ch 11)\n317:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 2 (CRI), Ch 6 (CRDs, operator pattern, HPA concept), Ch 9 (CNI), Ch 12 (mTLS, zero trust, policy engines), Ch 14 (charts as operator delivery), Ch 15 (declarative delivery)\n319:- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. Mandatory anchors: **CRI (Ch 2), CNI (Ch 9), CSI (Ch 11), CRDs (Ch 6)** — the four-way retrieval that *is* the secondary Zenith; **release cadence paired with version skew (Ch 8)** — the second fix for the Ch 8 decay problem, where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other; **unschedulable Pods (Ch 7)** as Cluster Autoscaler's trigger — the second fix for the Ch 7 decay problem; **metrics-server (Ch 13)** as HPA's input; **VPA not shipped by default** retrieved by name as \"the object exists but nothing happens without the component\" (Ch 10); Helm-delivered CRDs (Ch 14)\n334:- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]** — last content chapter, most accumulated decay, so the schedule closes at the ceiling. Draws from all previous. Mandatory anchors: **metrics-server vs a monitoring system (Ch 13)** — the distinction the exam actually tests; probes (Ch 5) as health-checking that is *not* observability; requests/limits (Ch 5) as the numbers metrics report against; mesh telemetry (Ch 17); node-level log agents as DaemonSets (Ch 6)\n417:| 11 | 20% | 6–10 | StatefulSet loop closed; ConfigMap/Secret as volumes | **Ch 5 Pod volumes / Ch 6 StatefulSet** |\n420:| 14 | 20% | 9–13 | Ch 6 rollback vs Helm rollback | **Ch 4 `apply`; Ch 6 workload resources** |\n421:| 15 | **25%** | all | **Control loop (Ch 3) — the Zenith**; rolling-update mechanics (Ch 6); Helm as Argo input (Ch 14); ConfigMap/Secret as factor III (Ch 4) | Ch 3, 4, 6 |\n422:| 16 | **25%** | all | Ch 13 handoff; Pod phases (Ch 5); Service→EndpointSlice (Ch 9); StatefulSet+PVC (Ch 6, 11) | Ch 5, 6, 9 |\n424:| 18 | **25%** | all | metrics-server vs monitoring (Ch 13); probes ≠ observability (Ch 5); log agents as DaemonSets (Ch 6); mesh telemetry (Ch 17) | Ch 5, 6, 13 |\n434:| Ch 6 ↔ Ch 11 | StatefulSet introduced / PV pairing completed — the book's one deliberate forward reference |\n438:| Ch 3 → Ch 6 → Ch 15 | The control-loop spine, three beats |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d82112b-22f6-4428-bc5d-c2e0b4ec1a25","uuid":"8b94d557-9f98-43b6-b412-099c5e2c8943","timestamp":"2026-08-24T11:41:17.708Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"29:**Also flagged:** B3 and B4 disagree on the early spacing ramp. B3 says Ch 3 = 10%, Ch 4 = 15%, then 20–25%. B4's restatement says Ch 4–5 ≈ 10%, Ch 6–8 ≈ 15%, Ch 9–13 ≈ 20%. B3 is the dedicated stage and its schedule is adopted here; B4's table is a generic r